"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[86757],{686757:n=>{n.exports="# Tableau Calculation to SQL Conversion Patterns\n\nComprehensive mapping of Tableau calculated field expressions to Databricks SQL for metric view migration.\n\n## Migration Status Summary\n\n| Calculation Type                        | Migration Status | Notes                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------------------------- | ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Row-Level Calculations                  | ✅ Supported     | Direct SQL expression                                                                                     |\n| Aggregate Calculations                  | ✅ Supported     | SQL aggregate function                                                                                    |\n| LOD Expressions (FIXED/INCLUDE/EXCLUDE) | ⚠️ Partial       | FIXED/TABLE_SCOPED via source SQL; EXCLUDE via window measures; INCLUDE skipped. See `lod_expressions.md` |\n| Table Calculations                      | ⚠️ Partial       | TOTAL/RUNNING_SUM supported via window measures; others skipped. See `window_measures.md`                 |\n| Parameter References                    | ❌ Not Supported | Skip with documentation; metric view YAML cannot reference parameters                                     |\n\n## Critical Migration Rules\n\nBefore converting any calculation, apply these rules:\n\n1. **Field Reference Conversion:** Remove brackets, convert spaces to underscores or use backticks\n   - `[Field Name]` → `Field_Name` or `` `Field Name` ``\n\n2. **String Quote Conversion:** Double quotes → Single quotes\n   - `\"Hello\"` → `'Hello'`\n\n3. **String Concatenation:** MUST convert `+` to `CONCAT()` or `||` operator\n   - `[str1] + [str2]` → `CONCAT(str1, str2)` or `str1 || str2`\n\n---\n\n## MEASURE() Composition Constraint\n\n`MEASURE()` cannot be passed to **any** SQL function — only `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` work between `MEASURE()` calls. Wrapping (e.g., `NULLIF(MEASURE(x), 0)`, `try_divide(MEASURE(a), MEASURE(b))`, `COALESCE(MEASURE(x), 0)`) raises `INTERNAL_ERROR`. Raw SQL aggregations inside `expr:` / `source:` (e.g., `SUM(x) / SUM(y)`) have no such restriction.\n\n```yaml\n# Composed measure — only arithmetic between MEASURE() calls\nexpr: MEASURE(total_profit) / MEASURE(total_sales)\n\n# Raw aggregation — any SQL function allowed\nexpr: SUM(profit) / SUM(sales)\n```\n\nFor general MEASURE() composition rules and patterns, see the metric views skill.\n\n---\n\n## Tableau Value Aliases\n\nTableau supports per-value aliases on dimensions (e.g., null → \"FIS\", \"lsc\" → \"Envisant\"). Convert these to a `CASE` expression on the dimension:\n\n```yaml\ndimensions:\n  - name: league_name_display\n    expr: |\n      CASE\n        WHEN NULLIF(TRIM(league_name), '') IS NULL THEN 'FIS'\n        WHEN LOWER(TRIM(league_name)) = 'lsc' THEN 'Envisant'\n        WHEN LOWER(TRIM(league_name)) = 'tcm' THEN 'TCM'\n        ELSE TRIM(league_name)\n      END\n```\n\nUse `LOWER(TRIM(...))` on both sides for case- and whitespace-insensitive matching, and treat empty strings as NULL via `NULLIF(TRIM(x), '')`. Find aliases in the workbook with `//column[@name=$col]/aliases/alias` (see `xml_extraction.md`).\n\n---\n\n## Aggregation Functions\n\n```yaml\naggregations:\n  - tableau: SUM([field])\n    sql: SUM(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: COUNT([field])\n    sql: COUNT(field)\n    notes: Counts non-null values\n\n  - tableau: COUNTD([field])\n    sql: COUNT(DISTINCT field)\n    notes: Different syntax - add DISTINCT keyword\n\n  - tableau: AVG([field])\n    sql: AVG(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: MIN([field])\n    sql: MIN(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: MAX([field])\n    sql: MAX(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: MEDIAN([field])\n    sql: PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY field)\n    notes: Databricks also supports native MEDIAN() function; use PERCENTILE_APPROX for large datasets\n\n  - tableau: ATTR([field])\n    sql: MAX(field)\n    notes: |\n      Approximation assuming single value.\n      Strict equivalent: CASE WHEN MIN(field) = MAX(field) THEN MAX(field) ELSE NULL END\n\n  - tableau: STDEV([field])\n    sql: STDDEV_SAMP(field)\n    notes: Sample standard deviation\n\n  - tableau: STDEVP([field])\n    sql: STDDEV_POP(field)\n    notes: Population standard deviation\n\n  - tableau: VAR([field])\n    sql: VAR_SAMP(field)\n    notes: Sample variance\n\n  - tableau: VARP([field])\n    sql: VAR_POP(field)\n    notes: Population variance\n\n  - tableau: CORR([x], [y])\n    sql: CORR(x, y)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: COVAR([x], [y])\n    sql: COVAR_SAMP(x, y)\n    notes: Sample covariance\n\n  - tableau: COVARP([x], [y])\n    sql: COVAR_POP(x, y)\n    notes: Population covariance\n\n  - tableau: PERCENTILE([field], p)\n    sql: PERCENTILE_CONT(p) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY field)\n    notes: Percentile computation\n\n  - tableau: COLLECT([field])\n    sql: COLLECT_LIST(field)\n    notes: Aggregates into array\n```\n\n---\n\n## Date Functions\n\n```yaml\ndate_functions:\n  # DATEDIFF - CRITICAL: Argument order differs\n  - tableau: DATEDIFF('day', [start], [end])\n    sql: DATEDIFF(end, start)\n    notes: 'CRITICAL: Arguments REVERSED for days (2-arg form)'\n    example:\n      tableau: DATEDIFF('day', [Start Date], [End Date])\n      sql: DATEDIFF(End_Date, Start_Date)\n\n  - tableau: DATEDIFF('month', [start], [end])\n    sql: FLOOR(MONTHS_BETWEEN(end, start))\n    notes: Or use DATEDIFF(MONTH, start, end) 3-arg form\n\n  - tableau: DATEDIFF('year', [start], [end])\n    sql: DATEDIFF(YEAR, start, end)\n    notes: 3-arg form, unit unquoted\n\n  - tableau: DATEDIFF('week', [start], [end])\n    sql: DATEDIFF(WEEK, start, end)\n    notes: 3-arg form\n\n  - tableau: DATEDIFF('quarter', [start], [end])\n    sql: DATEDIFF(QUARTER, start, end)\n    notes: 3-arg form\n\n  # DATEADD - Different functions by unit\n  - tableau: DATEADD('day', N, [date])\n    sql: DATE_ADD(date, N)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  - tableau: DATEADD('month', N, [date])\n    sql: ADD_MONTHS(date, N)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  - tableau: DATEADD('year', N, [date])\n    sql: ADD_MONTHS(date, N * 12)\n    notes: Multiply months by 12\n\n  - tableau: DATEADD('week', N, [date])\n    sql: DATE_ADD(date, N * 7)\n    notes: Multiply days by 7\n\n  # DATETRUNC - Same syntax\n  - tableau: DATETRUNC('month', [date])\n    sql: DATE_TRUNC('month', date)\n    notes: Same syntax\n\n  - tableau: DATETRUNC('year', [date])\n    sql: DATE_TRUNC('year', date)\n    notes: Same syntax\n\n  - tableau: DATETRUNC('quarter', [date])\n    sql: DATE_TRUNC('quarter', date)\n    notes: Same syntax\n\n  - tableau: DATETRUNC('week', [date])\n    sql: DATE_TRUNC('week', date)\n    notes: Same syntax\n\n  # DATEPART - Different syntax\n  - tableau: DATEPART('year', [date])\n    sql: YEAR(date)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('month', [date])\n    sql: MONTH(date)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('day', [date])\n    sql: DAY(date)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('quarter', [date])\n    sql: QUARTER(date)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('weekday', [date])\n    sql: DAYOFWEEK(date)\n    notes: Different function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('week', [date])\n    sql: WEEKOFYEAR(date)\n    notes: Different function; ISO-8601 week\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('dayofyear', [date])\n    sql: DAYOFYEAR(date)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('hour', [datetime])\n    sql: HOUR(datetime)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('minute', [datetime])\n    sql: MINUTE(datetime)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  - tableau: DATEPART('second', [datetime])\n    sql: SECOND(datetime)\n    notes: Use extraction function\n\n  # DATENAME - Use DATE_FORMAT\n  - tableau: DATENAME('month', [date])\n    sql: DATE_FORMAT(date, 'MMMM')\n    notes: Full month name (January)\n\n  - tableau: DATENAME('weekday', [date])\n    sql: DATE_FORMAT(date, 'EEEE')\n    notes: Full weekday name (Monday)\n\n  # Simple extraction functions\n  - tableau: YEAR([date])\n    sql: YEAR(date)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: MONTH([date])\n    sql: MONTH(date)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: DAY([date])\n    sql: DAY(date)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: QUARTER([date])\n    sql: QUARTER(date)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: WEEK([date])\n    sql: WEEKOFYEAR(date)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  # Current date/time\n  - tableau: TODAY()\n    sql: CURRENT_DATE()\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  - tableau: NOW()\n    sql: CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  # Construction\n  - tableau: MAKEDATE(y, m, d)\n    sql: MAKE_DATE(y, m, d)\n    notes: Same syntax\n\n  - tableau: MAKEDATETIME([date], [time])\n    sql: CAST(CONCAT(date, ' ', time) AS TIMESTAMP)\n    notes: Complex conversion\n\n  # Validation\n  - tableau: ISDATE([string])\n    sql: TRY_CAST(string AS DATE) IS NOT NULL\n    notes: Different approach\n\n  # Type conversion\n  - tableau: DATE([datetime])\n    sql: DATE(datetime)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: DATETIME([expression])\n    sql: CAST(expression AS TIMESTAMP)\n    notes: Use CAST\n```\n\n---\n\n## String Functions\n\n```yaml\nstring_functions:\n  # CRITICAL: String concatenation\n  - tableau: '[str1] + [str2]'\n    sql: CONCAT(str1, str2)\n    notes: 'CRITICAL: Must convert + to CONCAT() or || operator for strings. Databricks does NOT support + for string concatenation.'\n    example:\n      tableau: \"[First Name] + ' ' + [Last Name]\"\n      sql: \"CONCAT(First_Name, ' ', Last_Name)\"\n      sql_alt: \"First_Name || ' ' || Last_Name\"\n\n  - tableau: CONCAT([s1], [s2], ...)\n    sql: CONCAT(s1, s2, ...)\n    notes: Direct mapping (variadic)\n\n  # Substring functions\n  - tableau: LEFT([field], n)\n    sql: LEFT(field, n)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: RIGHT([field], n)\n    sql: RIGHT(field, n)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: MID([field], start, len)\n    sql: SUBSTRING(field, start, len)\n    notes: Different function name; both 1-based indexing\n\n  # Length\n  - tableau: LEN([field])\n    sql: LENGTH(field)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  # Case conversion\n  - tableau: UPPER([field])\n    sql: UPPER(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: LOWER([field])\n    sql: LOWER(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: PROPER([field])\n    sql: INITCAP(field)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  # Trimming\n  - tableau: TRIM([field])\n    sql: TRIM(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: LTRIM([field])\n    sql: LTRIM(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: RTRIM([field])\n    sql: RTRIM(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  # Replace\n  - tableau: REPLACE([s], [old], [new])\n    sql: REPLACE(s, old, new)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  # Search and contains\n  - tableau: CONTAINS([str], [sub])\n    sql: CONTAINS(str, sub)\n    notes: Direct mapping; or use str LIKE '%' || sub || '%'\n\n  - tableau: STARTSWITH([s], [prefix])\n    sql: STARTSWITH(s, prefix)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ENDSWITH([s], [suffix])\n    sql: ENDSWITH(s, suffix)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: FIND([str], [sub])\n    sql: POSITION(sub IN str)\n    notes: Different syntax; or use LOCATE(sub, str)\n\n  - tableau: FIND([str], [sub], start)\n    sql: LOCATE(sub, str, start)\n    notes: Different function\n\n  - tableau: FINDNTH([str], [sub], n)\n    sql: N/A\n    notes: No direct equivalent; requires complex workaround\n\n  # Split\n  - tableau: SPLIT([str], delim, n)\n    sql: SPLIT_PART(str, delim, n)\n    notes: Both use 1-based indexing\n\n  # Character functions\n  - tableau: ASCII([str])\n    sql: ASCII(str)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: CHAR(n)\n    sql: CHR(n)\n    notes: Different function name\n\n  - tableau: SPACE(n)\n    sql: REPEAT(' ', n)\n    notes: Different approach\n\n  # Row-level MIN/MAX for strings\n  - tableau: MIN([str1], [str2])\n    sql: LEAST(str1, str2)\n    notes: Different function; row-level comparison\n\n  - tableau: MAX([str1], [str2])\n    sql: GREATEST(str1, str2)\n    notes: Different function; row-level comparison\n\n  # Regular expressions\n  - tableau: REGEXP_MATCH([str], pattern)\n    sql: REGEXP(str, pattern)\n    notes: Direct mapping; escape backslashes in SQL\n\n  - tableau: REGEXP_REPLACE([str], pattern, replacement)\n    sql: REGEXP_REPLACE(str, pattern, replacement)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: REGEXP_EXTRACT([str], pattern)\n    sql: REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pattern, 0)\n    notes: Add group index (0 for entire match)\n\n  - tableau: REGEXP_EXTRACT_NTH([str], pattern, n)\n    sql: REGEXP_EXTRACT(str, pattern, n)\n    notes: Group is 0-indexed in Tableau, add appropriate index in Databricks\n```\n\n---\n\n## Logical Functions\n\n```yaml\nlogical_functions:\n  # IF/THEN/ELSE - Structural transformation\n  - tableau: IF [cond] THEN [a] ELSE [b] END\n    sql: CASE WHEN cond THEN a ELSE b END\n    notes: Different syntax\n    example:\n      tableau: IF [Sales] > 1000 THEN 'High' ELSE 'Low' END\n      sql: \"CASE WHEN Sales > 1000 THEN 'High' ELSE 'Low' END\"\n\n  - tableau: IF [c1] THEN [a] ELSEIF [c2] THEN [b] ELSE [c] END\n    sql: CASE WHEN c1 THEN a WHEN c2 THEN b ELSE c END\n    notes: ELSEIF becomes WHEN\n\n  # IIF - Spelling difference\n  - tableau: IIF([cond], [true], [false])\n    sql: IFF(cond, true, false)\n    notes: \"CRITICAL: Databricks uses IFF (two F's)\"\n\n  - tableau: IIF([cond], [true], [false], [unknown])\n    sql: CASE WHEN cond THEN true WHEN cond IS NULL THEN unknown ELSE false END\n    notes: 4-arg form with unknown handling\n\n  # CASE expressions\n  - tableau: CASE [field] WHEN [v1] THEN [r1] ELSE [r2] END\n    sql: CASE field WHEN v1 THEN r1 ELSE r2 END\n    notes: Same syntax (simple CASE)\n\n  - tableau: CASE WHEN [cond] THEN [r] END\n    sql: CASE WHEN cond THEN r END\n    notes: Same syntax (searched CASE)\n\n  # NULL handling\n  - tableau: IFNULL([field], [default])\n    sql: COALESCE(field, default)\n    notes: Use COALESCE for broader compatibility\n\n  - tableau: ZN([field])\n    sql: COALESCE(field, 0)\n    notes: Zero-null replacement\n\n  - tableau: ISNULL([field])\n    sql: field IS NULL\n    notes: Function to predicate syntax\n\n  - tableau: ISNOTNULL([field])\n    sql: field IS NOT NULL\n    notes: Function to predicate syntax\n\n  # Logical operators\n  - tableau: '[a] AND [b]'\n    sql: a AND b\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: '[a] OR [b]'\n    sql: a OR b\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: NOT [a]\n    sql: NOT a\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: '[field] IN ([v1], [v2])'\n    sql: field IN (v1, v2)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n```\n\n---\n\n## Number Functions\n\n```yaml\nnumber_functions:\n  # Rounding\n  - tableau: ROUND([field])\n    sql: ROUND(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ROUND([field], n)\n    sql: ROUND(field, n)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: CEILING([field])\n    sql: CEIL(field)\n    notes: Different spelling; Databricks supports both CEIL and CEILING as synonyms\n\n  - tableau: FLOOR([field])\n    sql: FLOOR(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: INT([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS INT)\n    notes: Use CAST; or FLOOR for truncation\n\n  # Powers and roots\n  - tableau: POWER([base], [exp])\n    sql: POWER(base, exp)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: SQRT([field])\n    sql: SQRT(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: SQUARE([field])\n    sql: POWER(field, 2)\n    notes: No direct SQUARE function\n\n  - tableau: '[base] ^ [exp]'\n    sql: POWER(base, exp)\n    notes: Exponentiation operator to function\n\n  # Logarithms - CRITICAL differences\n  - tableau: LN([field])\n    sql: LN(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping (natural log)\n\n  - tableau: LOG([field])\n    sql: LOG10(field)\n    notes: 'CRITICAL: Tableau LOG is base-10, not natural'\n\n  - tableau: LOG([field], [base])\n    sql: LOG(base, field)\n    notes: 'CRITICAL: Arguments REVERSED - Tableau: LOG(value, base), Databricks: LOG(base, value)'\n    example:\n      tableau: LOG(100, 10) # Returns 2\n      sql: LOG(10, 100) # Returns 2\n\n  - tableau: EXP([field])\n    sql: EXP(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  # Absolute and sign\n  - tableau: ABS([field])\n    sql: ABS(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: SIGN([field])\n    sql: SIGN(field)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  # Division and modulo\n  - tableau: DIV([num], [denom])\n    sql: FLOOR(num / denom)\n    notes: Integer division\n\n  - tableau: '[a] % [b]'\n    sql: a % b\n    notes: Direct mapping; or MOD(a, b)\n\n  # Trigonometric\n  - tableau: PI()\n    sql: PI()\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: RADIANS([degrees])\n    sql: RADIANS(degrees)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: DEGREES([radians])\n    sql: DEGREES(radians)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: SIN([radians])\n    sql: SIN(radians)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: COS([radians])\n    sql: COS(radians)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: TAN([radians])\n    sql: TAN(radians)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ASIN([value])\n    sql: ASIN(value)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ACOS([value])\n    sql: ACOS(value)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ATAN([value])\n    sql: ATAN(value)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: ATAN2([y], [x])\n    sql: ATAN2(y, x)\n    notes: Direct mapping\n\n  - tableau: COT([radians])\n    sql: 1 / TAN(radians)\n    notes: No direct function\n\n  # Row-level MIN/MAX for numbers\n  - tableau: MIN([a], [b])\n    sql: LEAST(a, b)\n    notes: Different function; row-level comparison\n\n  - tableau: MAX([a], [b])\n    sql: GREATEST(a, b)\n    notes: Different function; row-level comparison\n\n  # Random\n  - tableau: RANDOM()\n    sql: RAND()\n    notes: Different name\n```\n\n---\n\n## Type Conversion Functions\n\n```yaml\ntype_conversion:\n  - tableau: INT([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS INT)\n    notes: Truncates toward zero\n\n  - tableau: FLOAT([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS DOUBLE)\n    notes: Convert to floating point\n\n  - tableau: STR([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS STRING)\n    notes: Convert to string\n\n  - tableau: DATE([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS DATE)\n    notes: Convert to date\n\n  - tableau: DATETIME([field])\n    sql: CAST(field AS TIMESTAMP)\n    notes: Convert to timestamp\n```\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## LOD Expressions — Partially Supported\n\n**Status: ⚠️ PARTIALLY SUPPORTED (~40-55% migratable)**\n\nFull migration guide: see `lod_expressions.md`.\n\n| LOD Type             | Mechanism                                                        | Status                      |\n| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| FIXED / TABLE_SCOPED | `SOURCE_SQL_OVER` — pre-compute via `AGG() OVER()` in source SQL | ⚠️ Partial                  |\n| EXCLUDE              | `WINDOW_MEASURE` — YAML window measure with `range: all`         | ⚠️ Partial                  |\n| INCLUDE              | SKIP                                                             | ❌ No Databricks equivalent |\n\n---\n\n## Table Calculations\n\n`TOTAL` and `RUNNING_SUM` are migrated via window measures. Every other table-calc function (`RANK`, `RANK_DENSE`, `RANK_UNIQUE`, `INDEX`, `FIRST`, `LAST`, `SIZE`, `LOOKUP`, `PREVIOUS_VALUE`, `RUNNING_AVG`, `RUNNING_MIN`, `RUNNING_MAX`, `RUNNING_COUNT`, `WINDOW_*`) is skipped with documentation. See `window_measures.md` for the supported patterns.\n\n---\n\n## Parameter References\n\n**Status: ❌ Not supported.** Metric view YAML cannot reference parameters — any calculation containing `[Parameters.X]` is skipped with documentation in the metric view's `comment:` field.\n\n---\n\n## Calculation Type Classification\n\nClassify each Tableau calculation by inspecting the formula text in this order — first match wins:\n\n1. **Table calculation** — formula contains any of: `RANK`, `RANK_DENSE`, `RANK_MODIFIED`, `RANK_PERCENTILE`, `RANK_UNIQUE`, `INDEX`, `FIRST`, `LAST`, `SIZE`, `LOOKUP`, `PREVIOUS_VALUE`, `RUNNING_SUM`, `RUNNING_AVG`, `RUNNING_MIN`, `RUNNING_MAX`, `RUNNING_COUNT`, `WINDOW_SUM`, `WINDOW_AVG`, `WINDOW_MIN`, `WINDOW_MAX`, `WINDOW_COUNT`, `WINDOW_VAR`, `WINDOW_STDEV`, `WINDOW_COVAR`, `WINDOW_CORR`, `TOTAL`, `WINDOW_MEDIAN`, `WINDOW_PERCENTILE`. → Apply the Table Calculation decision tree (TOTAL / RUNNING_SUM only are migratable; the rest skip).\n2. **LOD expression** — formula matches `{(FIXED|INCLUDE|EXCLUDE)? ... : ... }`. → See `lod_expressions.md`.\n3. **Aggregate** — formula calls `SUM`, `COUNT`, `COUNTD`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `MEDIAN`, `ATTR`, `STDEV`, `STDEVP`, `VAR`, `VARP`, `CORR`, `COVAR`, `COVARP`, `PERCENTILE`, or `COLLECT`. → Direct SQL aggregate translation.\n4. **Row-level** — none of the above. → Direct SQL expression translation.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Family Gotchas\n\nA few conversions repeat across function families and are common error sources:\n\n- **IIF vs IFF**: Databricks uses two F's — `IFF()`. (`IF()` is also valid in Databricks.)\n- **String concatenation**: `+` is NOT a string concat operator in Databricks — convert to `CONCAT()` or `||`.\n"}}]);
